And there's been a lot of noise lately about, oh, Facebook's just going or Meta is just going to take all your assets and figure everything out and just, you know, 100% automate all your.

27
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:27,200
It's going to automatize all your ads at some point in the next, like, 18 months.

28
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:29,680
It's going to generate human looking people.

29
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:32,800
It's going to write your taglines for you.

30
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:34,400
It's going to just do everything.

31
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:36,960
It's going to customize it to every individual.

32
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:40,640
And it's just going to be this, like, utopia of ads.

33
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:44,160
And that's the take I've been hearing from a lot of folks.

34
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:49,600
But I saw a tweet from you recently with a bit of a contrarian spin on that.

35
00:02:50,160 --> 00:03:03,680
And so I'd love to hear how you think about using AI in creative generation in just in the entire ad creation process and kind of what to watch out for and where you think things are heading.

36
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:09,120
There's a lot of focus on using AI specifically for this, like for producing creatives.

37
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:17,600
And I think that's actually a mistake in the sense that it's hiding the fact that we can use AI for a number of other things that are not creative production.

38
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:28,960
Of course, it's a natural because of the cost of production, but also like of ideation as well, and how we can make multiple violence much easier than before.

39
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:34,000
So it's a little bit of it's so obvious that 95% of the discussion is there.

40
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:36,720
But my first answer would be maybe we haven't.

41
00:03:38,080 --> 00:03:39,360
done that much elsewhere.

42
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:43,840
And I think especially in creative analysis is where there's a bit of a gap.

43
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:49,120
I'm seeing a couple of tools coming up and some people, but it's still a rare conversation.

44
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:53,920
Like a lot of the focus is on creative production and that's kind of expected in a way.

45
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:59,920
I think if we look back since GPT came out, like early on, obviously it was like mostly text.

46
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:02,080
And then we got like mid journey and deli.

47
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:10,320
But like static pictures, at least for apps, is a relatively small amount of the ads that are actually thrown out there.

48
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:21,680
And text is important, especially on Google and elsewhere, but like for paid social, for meta, for TikTok and for gaming and networks, like text is a very small to insignificant part.

49
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:29,200
So obviously the acceleration has mostly been around the last six, twelve months, which is where video creation became really possible.

50
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:38,400
I mean, until a year ago, we're in summer 2025, until last summer, the quality of AI production for video was not good enough for ad creation.

51
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:53,040
And then as everything AI, it sort of accelerated so massively that I don't think I know a single team who's not using any AI tool for ad production, which doesn't mean that 100% of ads are AI generated yet.

52
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:57,760
And I don't think we'll ever reach 100% unless one platform decides that there's no other choice.

53
00:04:57,920 --> 00:04:59,120
But I don't think it's going to be it.

54
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:14,160
I think it's going to be a bit of a mix and probably it's going to be the majority, but it doesn't mean it's going to be the entirety, especially around creation, like, I mean, you can use it not just to generate the picture itself, but also to generate like the briefings.

55
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:17,840
And I mean, multiple variants is another use.

56
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:28,160
So within creative production, there's like not like so it could be to create value, could be to find new ideas.

57
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:34,960
And then sometimes you'd use several tools in a row, like one to generate ideas and one to classify them and try them.

58
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:36,960
And then later one to produce them.

59
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:39,120
There's one that's very exciting.

60
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:42,960
I saw the message last week and I thought, oh crazy.

61
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:46,160
I I remember writing something about it six years ago.

62
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:47,520
So pre GPT.

63
00:05:47,840 --> 00:05:56,240
So it was kind of funny, which was like, oh, it's the first time ever there's a tool that enables you to actually test a creative without spending a single dollar.

64
00:05:56,720 --> 00:06:12,480
And there's basically this pool of artificial humans that are used, they're not humans, they're just AI generated to classify if an ad has a chance to become a winner without having to spend any money, which is the dream.

65
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:16,640
Because I mean, on one side, we need to produce a lot of stuff to find out what works.

66
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:24,320
And the stars out there are like roughly, you have to produce fifty creative to have one that is actually a winner.

67
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:27,760
And this winner is going to take niney plus percent of the ad spend.

68
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:32,320
That's kind of a normal winner takes all mechanics that happens in ads.

69
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:36,000
So obviously create because when you need fifty to find one.

70
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:43,680
And sometimes more teams accelerating of creating 100 a week, thousands every month to find a couple of winners.

71
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:46,000
And so that's the WSUs.

72
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:56,000
But having something that tells you without having to do creative testing, which one has the most chance to win, is something that is still very nascent, very pioneer.

73
00:06:56,240 --> 00:07:01,680
And I don't think it's going to fully replace proper creative testing where you put a little bit of money behind it.

74
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:06,400
But it's definitely one way I'm looking forward to acceleration in the next year.

75
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:09,680
Like, we're not there yet, but it's something I can see it's coming.

76
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:23,600
In analysis as well, I think it's going to accelerate, especially around patterns like which hooks have always worked and which not, then you can come up and it's it's sort of all entangled into accelerating because of these different layers.

77
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:27,360
One accelerates the next one and amplifies, let's say, the next one.

78
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:28,480
It's all very exciting.

79
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:42,800
As of Meta making, and Mark Zuckerberg in particular making a declaration that his goal is that there's no more assets at all, that it's all generated, I think was voluntarily provocative about the direction it's taking.

80
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:49,600
I don't think we're going to get there in the next one, two, three years, and certainly not eighteen months, but it's certainly the directionction it's taking.

81
00:07:49,760 --> 00:07:53,920
I mean, Google has done a lot of it early with UIC and is still doing a lot of it.

82
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:57,840
Meta has done some of it and is clearly now accelerating.

83
00:07:58,320 --> 00:08:15,760
And I can see why this declaration, I mean, Zuckerberg said it in a way to prepare people that it's coming, not necessarily that this is the ultimate form that he talks about is what will be there in eighteen months, but it's pretty logical, like in terms of personalizing parts of the creative.

84
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:22,560
Like you could find, for example, a script of a creative, like something that fairly resonates with a lot of your audience.

85
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:34,960
But then if you magnify it that the text can be customized, that the character in the ad can be customized and someone would add something, someone looking more like them or a little bit older, and then you can cater to even more audience.

86
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:36,480
That makes a lot of sense.

87
00:08:37,040 --> 00:08:44,800
I'm seeing this happening today in a half manual way, which is like, oh, I've got something that wins, let's produce fifty of them.

88
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:51,280
And one with an older guy and one with a black woman and one with a twenty year old and one with whatever.

89
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:52,640
And you just try them, right?

90
00:08:53,040 --> 00:09:01,920
But I can see how right now it's more like a marketer's idea of, oh, I've got something that wins, let's make variants out of it into the platform automating more of this.

91
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:05,920
And so in a ground scheme of things, it does make sense that we take that direction.

92
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:10,640
I don't think it's going to be ultimately the only way the creatives are produced either.

93
00:09:11,360 --> 00:09:13,760
Where do you see folks failing with this right now?

94
00:09:14,080 --> 00:09:16,320
Or what do you think the things to watch out for?

95
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:23,840
I mean, you kind of alluded to it that these tools that kind of pick the winners in advance maybe shouldn't be relied on one hundred percent.

96
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:34,800
But what other things are you seeing that you think teams are doing or customers, the clients that you work with have suggested that you're like, I don't think we're quite like, that's not the right direction yet.

97
00:09:35,200 --> 00:09:36,400
It's hard to pinpoint one.

98
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:48,000
One thing that is always a tricky balance, a tricky trade off is when you find winners, obviously they're so much better than the rest that you want to double down on this.

99
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:49,600
So you create a lot of value of it.

100
00:09:49,840 --> 00:09:54,960
And ultimately sometimes I look at some accounts and I'm like, okay, but all you've got there is one concept.

101
00:09:55,360 --> 00:09:58,400
Like, you've got a few ads, but it's more of the same.

102
00:09:58,960 --> 00:10:04,000
And sooner or later, this is going to go down and you have to prepare a lot more.

103
00:10:04,400 --> 00:10:12,560
So it's kind of like, instead of exploring so many more concepts and the majority will fail, like the trick between.

104
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:24,640
how much I double down on winners, but how much I prepare what's next, doing something that has more likelihood to fail in the short term, but more likelihood to be required in the long term, is very hard to balance.

105
00:10:25,120 --> 00:10:33,440
And obviously, everyone's short term looking, especially in the UA, you know, we look at the results from yesterday and last quarter already no longer exists.

106
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:50,000
So yeah, long term preparation of what's next and audience expansion, especially when you spend at scale, like if the account is relatively small, I mean, you can tap on the same concept over and over and with a few variants you can push it around months, but everything is acceleratedating even in ads.

107
00:10:50,240 --> 00:11:03,360
And I think this is especially visible in TikTok where the trends go so much faster, like around music, around the specific hooks, around the first seconds, around even concept of ads like that, really accelerating.

108
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:08,000
And so you can't just double down on stuff like just got to create.

109
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:12,640
I think like AI obviously enables us to go faster in like, oh, this works.

110
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:14,560
I'm going to produce a hundred version of it.

111
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:19,280
And even if in two weeks this concept is dead, I'm going to have used it in the meantime.

112
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:24,560
So even more useful for cases like on TikTok where fatigue is much faster than.

113
00:11:24,720 --> 00:11:32,720
than, for example, on YouTube where you probably still want to craft a lot more, less ads, but go deeper.

114
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:36,480
The other one, because the question was, well, where do you see team failing?

115
00:11:36,640 --> 00:11:45,360
And here, there's something that I don't want to accept, but I still have to, which is we don't understand why winning ads win.

116
00:11:45,920 --> 00:11:53,280
And so we have this tendency, Eric Seufert is the extremist about it, about don't even try to understand why it works.

117
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:54,560
It's just a waste of time.

118
00:11:54,800 --> 00:12:00,560
I'm not that far, but in a way, yeah, I've seen teams fail a lot on, I don't know, you'd use an AI tool.

119
00:12:00,800 --> 00:12:04,080
will say, oh, look, those are my 200 last creatives.

120
00:12:04,560 --> 00:12:05,440
Those three are the best.

121
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:06,720
Everything else fails.

122
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:07,440
Tell me why.

123
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:24,560
And here it's sort of like you will get a result and you will get some patterns, but I don't think those patterns push you to the next one where you can actually replicate it so easily, like the very specific details of psychology, how people react, the kind of audience that is there, it's really hard to explain.

124
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:31,440
So I'm still doing it and I still understand why people would do it because then for iteration it brings you to the next one.

125
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:36,560
You want to learn from what you've done to keep progressing and it's and it's natural and sometimes maybe.

126
00:12:36,640 --> 00:12:43,760
Maybe it helps, but honestly, it's a little bit of overstated in the sense it's very hard to understand why winning ads win.

127
00:12:44,080 --> 00:12:48,560
And our human criteria is not very good, but actually the LLMs, they're not very good either.

128
00:12:48,640 --> 00:12:55,440
Like they could spot a couple of patterns and all, but it's not going to make the next winning ad that much easier either.

129
00:12:56,080 --> 00:13:03,120
It's sad, but in a way it's more like, okay, let's mass produce and see what sticks, rather than let's try to really explain why it works.

130
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:08,880
And even ads from competitors, you'd be like trying to understand why they work.

131
00:13:09,280 --> 00:13:16,160
And whether you're doing it with a team and brainstorming or you're doing it through AI or probably the best of it is doing both.

132
00:13:16,800 --> 00:13:18,960
Still, the outcome is slim and pretty bad.

133
00:13:19,680 --> 00:13:24,880
Well, the next question I want to ask is what tools are you currently seeing success with?

134
00:13:25,040 --> 00:13:27,200
But that will turn this into like a five hour podcast.

135
00:13:27,600 --> 00:13:42,000
And so I was thinking as you were responding to that last question that we should just do a webinar like you, maybe Nathan Hudson, maybe Marcus Burke, get a few people who are really into this and do a whole webinar specifically on like what tools are working.

136
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:49,600
I think that's going to be changing so quickly that maybe doing it on podcasts is not as good as just doing it on a webinar and some blog posts that we can uplodate over time.

137
00:13:50,400 --> 00:13:57,200
But the next big topic I wanted to bring up was app to web, the Epic lawsuit, the injunction.

138
00:13:57,360 --> 00:14:00,800
If you're listening to this podcast surely you know, so we're not going to go over it all.

139
00:14:01,360 --> 00:14:08,080
But what are you seeing apps do currently and are you seeing things work in sending people from the app to the web?

140
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:21,840
And then as a precursor, you've probably been on vacation, so you probably haven't had a chance to listen, but the podcast episode just prior to this one, I talked to Zumba, and they are seeing success sending people to the web, and they've been doing a lot of experimentation.

141
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:29,680
And then as I'm also hearing from a ton of folks that they're scared to do it because they don't want Apple to stop featuring them.

142
00:14:29,920 --> 00:14:32,160
They don't want to get in a bad position with Apple.

143
00:14:33,280 --> 00:14:45,360
And so there seems to be like a lot of variance going on right now with whether you should even try sending people out of the app to the web, who you should send out of the app to the web, what experiments you should do.

144
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:53,840
So give us a quick take on august 2025, where are things at with app to web and where do you see things going?

145
00:14:54,960 --> 00:14:55,120
Sure.

146
00:14:55,760 --> 00:15:06,800
If I were to like super simplify, like if you got like a big brand or if you've got something that people already want from the get-go, it's much more likely that App to Web is going to work.

147
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:09,440
So if you're Spotify, it's obvious.

148
00:15:10,240 --> 00:15:15,920
Even Zoomba, I think the app is pretty recent, but it's the official app of Zoomba.

149
00:15:16,480 --> 00:15:20,640
And like, people already know what it is, like, there's a trust factor in there.

150
00:15:21,040 --> 00:15:32,720
If you're an indie developer and you're launching a brandless app last month, it's very likely that App to Web is a fool they're run in the sense that one, you can't do it all because you've got a small team or it's just you.

151
00:15:33,280 --> 00:15:39,200
Two, it's less likely to work because you don't have, like, this recognition, this brand, and people are less likely to like go.

152
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:42,320
And three, the analysis is actually quite tricky.

153
00:15:42,560 --> 00:15:44,960
So you need minimum resources to execute this.

154
00:15:45,200 --> 00:15:58,960
Minimum resources, not just in designing a great flow, which is like a requirement because a poor flow will make it not work where it could have, but actually analyzing properly and comparing the two is not as easy as it looks.

155
00:15:59,120 --> 00:16:03,600
Like refund works different, renewal works different, free trial works very different.

156
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:07,200
You need to deal with throw, like the amount of small detail.

157
00:16:07,520 --> 00:16:16,000
And one thing that is really interesting from the folks for whom it does work to send people outside of the app is that typically they don't sell the same plans.

158
00:16:16,160 --> 00:16:29,120
Like what works on an app to web flow is much closer to what you see in a web to app flow, like where the first touch point is on the web and then you charge and then only they go to the app that what you see through IAPs.

159
00:16:29,680 --> 00:16:47,680
And so if you port the exact same flow and your analysis level is not deep enough to understand all the small details that are going to change, you could actually be misfiring and believe you're doing something great when it's not or vice versa or actually missing out on it's not as easy as it looks.

160
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:48,720
So my big advice is.

161
00:16:49,200 --> 00:16:54,000
that if you're big and you have the resource and you've got the brand, sure go try it like right now.

162
00:16:54,320 --> 00:16:57,280
If you're very small and it's just you, don't even try.

163
00:16:57,440 --> 00:16:58,240
It's not worth it.

164
00:16:58,400 --> 00:17:03,440
You're only paying fifty percent and the complexity, you're probably better off investing your effort elsewhere.

165
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:10,880
But obviously, the majority of people will land somewhere between and it's at which time do we make the choice to actually experiment with this?

166
00:17:11,600 --> 00:17:17,920
And curiously, the teams I worked the closest with, they decided not to even test.

167
00:17:18,240 --> 00:17:19,280
Like, I was like, why?

168
00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:26,000
I was like, because prioritization is saying no to great ideas, no saying no to stupid ideas and we can't do it all.

169
00:17:26,240 --> 00:17:31,840
And we've got this road map for the next three, six months where we've got like very big projects going on.

170
00:17:32,160 --> 00:17:37,760
We're going to wait for the dust to settle because then there was the other appeal and then we didn't know how Apple would react.

171
00:17:37,920 --> 00:17:51,120
Like, I mean, now I think the dust starts to sell and we see more or less other examples and Apple might change things later, but for now they haven't taken like massive retaliation stuff.

172
00:17:51,360 --> 00:17:53,200
Sometimes there's a first mover advantage.

173
00:17:53,360 --> 00:17:58,960
Like, and for example, those who were advertising on TikTok early, obviously, they like it was a huge advantage.

174
00:17:59,280 --> 00:18:00,960
I'd rather have people from Tinder.

175
00:18:00,960 --> 00:18:09,040
People from teams that experiment a lot do it and then listen to what they do and shortcut to what they think is working rather because there's a lot to test there.

176
00:18:09,200 --> 00:18:16,320
Like, I mean, the different flows, the pricing, how do you charge, how do you prioritize the Apple payment versus not and so on.

177
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:18,320
Like, it's like there's a lot of factors.

178
00:18:18,400 --> 00:18:22,960
So the teams that I'm working with, they're like, yeah, we're looking at it.

179
00:18:23,280 --> 00:18:29,760
We're talking to people about it, but we're going to push it into the next step of the roadmap where we think we can execute faster then.

180
