00:37:02,880 --> 00:37:03,760
So what do you mean by that?

359
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:08,480
And then like, where do you see data being broken in ways?

360
00:37:08,880 --> 00:37:22,080
And you kind of already shared one, but what are the steps or what are the ste tools or what are the kind of basics that you should have in place for even monitoring to make sure that things don't break even when you think they're working?

361
00:37:22,640 --> 00:37:25,120
How do you figure out whether your data is good or not?

362
00:37:26,640 --> 00:37:33,360
It's always hard to prevent future breaks, but I know when someone tells me, like, hey, go, go check my ad account.

363
00:37:33,520 --> 00:37:35,200
Tell me what you think or make a note deal.

364
00:37:35,360 --> 00:37:39,680
I don't make so many of them, but like sometimes, maybe it's a friend, maybe it's a portfolio company, so let's still do it.

365
00:37:39,920 --> 00:37:41,840
And they say, oh, here's the ad manager.

366
00:37:41,920 --> 00:37:43,920
And I say, I don't care about the ad manager.

367
00:37:44,000 --> 00:37:45,040
Show me the event manager.

368
00:37:45,280 --> 00:37:47,520
Like Facebook calls its event manager in Google.

369
00:37:49,520 --> 00:37:50,480
That's the first place I look.

370
00:37:50,720 --> 00:37:52,000
I don't even look at the ad account.

371
00:37:52,240 --> 00:38:02,880
And the first thing I'm going to check is does the amount of conversion, which whatever this conversion is, so let's say it's going to be install and free trial and paid subscriptions to simplify.

372
00:38:03,360 --> 00:38:12,880
And I'm going to go check in the event manager, do the number that the platform reports being sent match more or less what we have internally?

373
00:38:13,440 --> 00:38:14,640
And very often it's not the case.

374
00:38:15,280 --> 00:38:19,920
I really don't care about the five percent discrepancy here and it's never going to match one hundred percent and that's okay.

375
00:38:20,000 --> 00:38:22,960
And there's a number of answers why it's not one hundred percent.

376
00:38:23,280 --> 00:38:23,840
It doesn't matter.

377
00:38:24,480 --> 00:38:27,600
But in so many cases, I'm seeing 30% or 50%.

378
00:38:27,840 --> 00:38:31,840
And this is going to be a major problem that I need to fix before seeing anything else.

379
00:38:32,000 --> 00:38:34,720
So I take Facebook as an example because it's a big example.

380
00:38:34,960 --> 00:38:39,680
But I'm going to go to the event manager and I'm going to say, okay, show me the last 30 days or last 7 days.

381
00:38:40,240 --> 00:38:41,360
How many installs did you see?

382
00:38:41,520 --> 00:38:44,640
How many free trial did you see in total without attribution?

383
00:38:45,040 --> 00:38:48,080
Not the ones that Facebook thinks were coming from the campaigns.

384
00:38:48,480 --> 00:38:55,520
Not the ones that Google attributes to this particular paid activity, but like what we're actually sending them because they report it there.

385
00:38:55,840 --> 00:39:03,520
And I'm going to compare it with something like RodeCard or Amplitude or MixPanel or whatever they have that is internal before we send it.

386
00:39:03,760 --> 00:39:07,040
If I'm seeing more than ten percent discrepancy here, it's going to be a problem.

387
00:39:07,280 --> 00:39:12,560
Those tools, they're not particularly sophisticated, but they're not particularly easy to understand either.

388
00:39:12,880 --> 00:39:20,160
So it's sometimes kind of tricky to match the dates, the geographies, the platform properly.

389
00:39:20,400 --> 00:39:25,520
And so to debug that actually the data I'm seeing there is the one that is supposed to be there.

390
00:39:25,920 --> 00:39:32,000
I remember making mistakes myself sometimes, like, I don't know, one case I was like, why am I seeing double the event on Facebook?

391
00:39:32,400 --> 00:39:42,880
How is this even possible that there's tw just that there are two sources that Meta managed to deduplicate, but that doesn't show up like this in the event manager.

392
00:39:43,200 --> 00:39:49,200
So the first step I do is what the platform is receiving, is it matching more or less what we think is happening?

393
00:39:49,360 --> 00:39:57,280
Because if that's not the case, well, after that they're going to try attributing to a campaign and there, there's a whole lot of other things that can happen.

394
00:39:57,760 --> 00:40:05,200
One that you don't trust attribution, which I would understand, but one that among those signals that you've sent, maybe some didn't have the right parameters.

395
00:40:05,840 --> 00:40:18,800
Maybe, I don't know, I remember making a change and yeah, we're like, oh, only twenty percent of the conversion are passing like 100% is passing to the event manager, but only 20% of what we think is happening is being reported at the ads.

396
00:40:19,200 --> 00:40:27,200
And actually, Meta couldn't attribute it on AEM and was only reporting the double opting, like the IDFA people, until we fixed it.

397
00:40:27,520 --> 00:40:35,360
And so, yeah, the step one is, is the toll actually matching because, and I'm saying it because if it's not, nothing else can work.

398
00:40:35,920 --> 00:40:37,680
And it's kind of an easy check to do.

399
00:40:37,840 --> 00:40:46,960
Like there can be many other problems, it's just one, but that it's so common and it's so blocking of doing anything that that that's always the first thing I'm checking.

400
00:40:47,200 --> 00:40:52,080
What drives nuts is that when it doesn't work, the amount of reason that can be for it not to work.

401
00:40:52,320 --> 00:40:53,440
But that's something you need to fix.

402
00:40:53,760 --> 00:40:54,320
Yes or yes.

403
00:40:54,480 --> 00:40:55,680
Like one way or another.

404
00:40:56,000 --> 00:40:57,200
That could be changing the source.

405
00:40:57,280 --> 00:41:01,520
That could be sending different parameters, changing the event entirely or whatever.

406
00:41:02,000 --> 00:41:13,040
It's also because the default configuration of, for example, the Meta SDK or basically you can put the Meta SDK and tell Meta, okay, just map my event on your own.

407
00:41:13,280 --> 00:41:18,000
And you have, like, the install, for example, is the hardest to debug because it's not an event.

408
00:41:18,240 --> 00:41:20,000
It's something that came pre-packaged.

409
00:41:20,160 --> 00:41:22,400
So when it doesn't work, you've got less levers to move.

410
00:41:22,720 --> 00:41:23,200
But, for example.

411
00:41:24,160 --> 00:41:27,200
The free try, the paid conversion can be mapped.

412
00:41:27,920 --> 00:41:37,280
Something that happens very often is that a direct purchase without a free trial and a free trial that converts to pay and a renewal would all be mapped to purchase together.

413
00:41:37,920 --> 00:41:52,880
How can you actually read anything when you've got this mixed bag of events, which I sometimes use a mixed bag of events for optimization, but as long as I understand what's actually passing through, and I'm going to want all of those individually to make sure, like, okay, I've unbugged that they work properly.

414
00:41:53,120 --> 00:41:57,520
Now I can start getting a little smarter around what I'm sending.

415
00:41:58,160 --> 00:42:06,080
The default configuration is not as easy as it looks, I think is one of the cases where making a little bit of effort because it's not something you have to work every other day.

416
00:42:06,240 --> 00:42:10,000
Like it's something you need to fix, but then you can run on this for six, twelve months.

417
00:42:10,320 --> 00:42:19,120
You can never forget about it for the reason I mentioned at the beginning, but it's something that you fix once in a while and also because it gives you options.

418
00:42:19,600 --> 00:42:35,040
Like I was working with an account where they had like massive success scaling on a couple different platforms, like really impressive trajectory went from zero to eight digits in a couple of years, like a very beautiful trajectory.

419
00:42:35,600 --> 00:42:43,280
And then at some point, things started working not as well, and we tried everything we could, like, you know, creative and fixing the event and so on.

420
00:42:43,840 --> 00:42:50,880
One of the problems is that they put all their eggs in one basket, and that basket worked great for a long time, but when it broke they had no alternative.

421
00:42:51,280 --> 00:43:07,520
And one of the reasons to have your signals properly handled is also to have optionality in the sense of if what works best for you is optimizing for free trial, so be it, or optimizing for value, like for revenue optimization, so be it, that's great, double down on it and spend ninety percent on it.

422
00:43:08,240 --> 00:43:11,600
But you probably also want to have the other options available.

423
00:43:12,160 --> 00:43:21,760
Sometimes because it's audience expansion and I would run an event optimization campaign towards free trial in parallel with a revenue optimization campaign and Facebook would bring different users to the app.

424
00:43:22,000 --> 00:43:28,640
So for scaling, but also for the many, many cases where things start to break, which is much more often than people think.

425
00:43:28,960 --> 00:43:33,280
So I'd rather have different options in my toolbox basically.

426
00:43:33,920 --> 00:43:34,720
Maybe not for today.

427
00:43:34,880 --> 00:43:37,360
If something works, yes, sure, let's go all in on it.

428
00:43:38,080 --> 00:43:42,400
But to have this for later, also to prep to experiment a little bit on the site.

429
00:43:44,000 --> 00:43:54,640
So one of the reasons I started talking about it, about this topic was one, I had the case, I had two cases in my portfolio where one clearly had their events broken, so we needed to fix it.

430
00:43:54,960 --> 00:44:00,400
And then we thought, if we're going to fix it, let's fix it and make it smarter at the same time.

431
00:44:00,720 --> 00:44:04,000
And then I had another client who worked with a third party.

432
00:44:04,480 --> 00:44:09,680
They're already doing some kind of optimization on their own and actually already a fairly sophisticated system.

433
00:44:10,160 --> 00:44:12,320
I thought, what if there's something I missed?

434
00:44:12,560 --> 00:44:15,760
Let's go and ask some of the best out there about what they're doing.

435
00:44:16,240 --> 00:44:18,000
And we actually handed it over to them.

436
00:44:18,320 --> 00:44:19,360
We're running two tests.

437
00:44:19,520 --> 00:44:20,960
One is looking good, the other not.

438
00:44:21,280 --> 00:44:30,640
So it's not completely, let's say, conclusive., but they definitely did stuff that we wouldn't have done on our own and go one step beyond.

439
00:44:30,880 --> 00:44:43,280
So these two things about fixing, getting smarter and going one step beyond, they all came at a moment where at the same time I felt that 95% of the debate on paid media was about creative production.

440
00:44:43,520 --> 00:44:46,720
I was like, sure, should be talked, but that's not the only topic.

441
00:44:46,960 --> 00:44:50,240
So I like to take my control in the hat and say, I'm going to talk about something else.

442
00:44:50,800 --> 00:44:54,160
Suddenly, I also noticed a lot more talks about that.

443
00:44:54,480 --> 00:44:58,880
So either really good timing or just people got inspired and worked on it.

444
00:44:59,040 --> 00:45:00,160
I think it was a little bit of both.

445
00:45:01,120 --> 00:45:02,960
I'm glad the topic has picked up a little bit.

446
00:45:03,280 --> 00:45:08,880
It doesn't mean that if you've got amazing signal engineering and you have one ad in your account, it's going to work.

447
00:45:09,600 --> 00:45:10,320
That's not going to work.

448
00:45:11,040 --> 00:45:11,120
Yeah.

449
00:45:12,640 --> 00:45:19,600
Before we move on to more specifics around how to do the signal engineering, I did want to ask one more question on the data.

450
00:45:20,480 --> 00:45:32,640
Again, just because these are the kind of questions I think people would be, you know, shouting into their car as they're driving around listening to the podcast is that how do you think about this when you're scaling on multiple networks?

451
00:45:33,280 --> 00:45:36,240
So you're doing Google and Meta and TikTok.

452
00:45:37,200 --> 00:45:46,320
Should each of those event managers be seeing 100% of your conversions, 100% of your free trials, 100% of everything?

453
00:45:46,960 --> 00:45:49,920
Is that what you're looking for in each individual event manager?

454
00:45:50,160 --> 00:45:58,400
Or are you looking for Google to be seeing just a percentage of the events and Meta to be seeing a percentage of the events?

455
00:45:58,960 --> 00:46:03,840
If you're early in this process, you probably want them all to receive 100%.

456
00:46:04,000 --> 00:46:07,440
Like you don't filter anything, you send 100% of these different events.

457
00:46:08,240 --> 00:46:08,320
Yes.

458
00:46:10,000 --> 00:46:14,960
It's almost normal that the same scenario is not going to be the best for every platform.

459
00:46:15,360 --> 00:46:26,400
And it's possible that you end up optimizing for revenue in Meta and for a qualified trial in Google and for a filter of direct purchase and trial conversion in TikTok, to say something.

460
00:46:26,640 --> 00:46:38,240
Like what's going to work is not going to be universal, like every network has different type of user but also different context but also different ways of optimizing, like of valuing the signals that you send them.

461
00:46:38,560 --> 00:46:40,240
And on top of that, they don't have the same source.

462
00:46:40,400 --> 00:46:48,720
You might be using an MMP and sending to all these sources, but it's also very possible that you're sending to Google via Firebase and to Facebook via the Facebook SDK.

463
00:46:48,720 --> 00:46:49,360
Facebook has declined and so on.

464
00:46:49,520 --> 00:46:54,880
So like the easy answer is yes, send 100% of the events to all the different platforms.

465
00:46:55,120 --> 00:47:00,800
In reality, one, they're going to be different sources, so they're different by nature and different things are going to work.

466
00:47:01,120 --> 00:47:11,920
So, for example, you might have to filter younger audience trials to TikTok, but you don't necessarily need to do it for Meta because it works differently.

467
00:47:12,240 --> 00:47:14,560
Like what I do is I map these different events.

468
00:47:14,640 --> 00:47:19,600
So let's say I've got my free trials, I send them all, but maybe I'm never using them in any campaign.

469
00:47:20,000 --> 00:47:29,680
And then as soon as I can verify the quality of the data that's being passed, and then I've got my qualified free tri trials or filtered free trials, let's say, being sent to the different networks, but I map it.

470
00:47:29,920 --> 00:47:32,480
When I say map, it's like I declare it as a different event.

471
00:47:32,800 --> 00:47:42,160
So maybe all the free trials are going to be called start trial, but then the qualified trial is going to be called whatever it's going to be maybe a purchase, maybe subscribe, maybe something.

472
00:47:42,400 --> 00:47:49,840
Actually, I wish that the platform offers more options in the number of events that they propose because probably because it's not so common.

473
00:47:50,240 --> 00:47:51,520
That's why the list is limited.

474
00:47:51,680 --> 00:47:55,680
And if we remember two or three years ago, Facebook didn't even have start trial and subscription.

475
00:47:55,840 --> 00:47:58,000
It was all purchased from gaming and e-commerce.

476
00:47:58,240 --> 00:48:00,560
So the baseline then is that as you start.

477
00:48:01,120 --> 00:48:13,520
to think about signal engineering, the first step is that 100% of your data is going to all the different networks and you just verify that you're within that like 5 to 10 percent threshold across all the different networks.

478
00:48:14,320 --> 00:48:30,560
Then you move on to starting to filter the events and then by doing so, then you have a better sense for what numbers should be showing up on each platform because then you can determine I'm not sending free trials of users under 25 to TikTok.

479
00:48:30,800 --> 00:48:35,600
And so you can do the math then of like, okay, we know that when I was sending 100 percent it was working.

480
00:48:35,680 --> 00:48:38,720
And so now I'm only sendinging 75%.

481
00:48:39,040 --> 00:48:44,240
And so now I have an idea of what should be arriving at that particular ad network.

482
00:48:44,640 --> 00:48:49,040
And then that's how you do your data validation once you start doing the signal engineering, right?

483
00:48:49,680 --> 00:48:50,000
Correct.

484
00:48:50,640 --> 00:48:58,480
I mean, all of this, well, I remember doing this in 2017, and it didn't come up from a brilliant idea in my mind.

485
00:48:58,640 --> 00:49:03,040
It just came up from the observation that my free trial rates on Google Ads was terrible.

486
00:49:03,280 --> 00:49:10,000
Like we would have, let's say, 40% trial to pay conversion on organic, on meta, like, I mean, small differences.

487
00:49:10,160 --> 00:49:11,680
And then we'd have like 15% on Google.

488
00:49:12,160 --> 00:49:13,920
I was like, what are we doing wrong here?

489
00:49:14,160 --> 00:49:22,640
I was like, okay, for Google, we're going to identify some of the reasons those trials are not converting and we're going to send them a different signal to try to fix it.

490
00:49:22,800 --> 00:49:36,960
It wasn't like, we're not trying to be smart and just do senior engineering, but we started from a problem initially, like maybe if you don't have this problem, maybe if your free trial quality coming from paid is great, you don't need to go further for now.

491
00:49:37,280 --> 00:49:41,440
Like maybe you could do even better, but like I started from a problem.

492
00:49:41,840 --> 00:49:48,000
And then seeing that this was moving the needle in ways, I said, okay, now I can get a little smarter.

493
00:49:48,720 --> 00:49:54,160
One of the next steps is that historically all these platforms offer event optimization.

494
00:49:54,400 --> 00:49:58,640
So they offer instant optimization, which honestly, you should never do.

495
00:49:58,800 --> 00:50:01,920
There are a couple of edge cases where you might, but in most cases, it's a very bad idea.

496
00:50:02,240 --> 00:50:09,200
There's event optimization, which can be different events, they can be filtered and so on, and we can talk a little bit more, and there's revenue optimization.

497
00:50:09,600 --> 00:50:16,000
And the problem with revenue optimization is that from the gaming world and e commerce world, this was just revenue.

498
00:50:16,240 --> 00:50:18,800
This was just revenue that was generated by the sale.

499
00:50:19,360 --> 00:50:24,000
But in subscription, because we've got the free trial and because we've got the renewals.

500
00:50:25,040 --> 00:50:30,400
The revenue that is actually happening that day is actually not what we need to send back to the platform.

501
00:50:30,960 --> 00:50:35,040
So I started getting smarter and say, okay, I'm going to engineer the revenue that I'm sending.

502
00:50:35,280 --> 00:50:38,880
So it's not a predicted LTV, it's a predicted value at day X.

503
00:50:39,120 --> 00:50:46,960
Maybe it's months two, maybe it's personally, I like to use months thirteen because I've got the first yearly renewal and I think it's a better comparison between monthly and yearly.

504
00:50:47,200 --> 00:50:48,960
But whatever, it doesn't matter what I like.

505
00:50:49,280 --> 00:51:02,160
The real revenue, the one that passes by default on the SDK, is not the one that I want to be sending because the free trial conversion is going to come too late because it's going to overvalue the yearly over the monthly, but maybe or, let's take the weekly.

506
00:51:02,400 --> 00:51:06,400
Sometimes the weekly, the LTV of a weekly is great because the price is so much higher.

507
00:51:06,720 --> 00:51:13,280
If someone renews for six months on a weekly plan, the revenue where we want to send to the platform is not the real one.

508
00:51:13,920 --> 00:51:22,960
It's going to typically platform will over index on yearly because all the revenue comes on day one, but maybe your weekly plan has a higher LTV when people renew along.

509
00:51:23,120 --> 00:51:26,800
So you're just telling the platform something that is wrong about what you're looking for.

510
00:51:27,040 --> 00:51:30,160
And here the idea is not only to trick.

511
00:51:30,320 --> 00:51:36,720
And I remember talking to Andre about senior engineering and they summarized it that, oh, Thomas is manipulating the ad platform.

512
00:51:37,120 --> 00:51:44,160
I'm like, no, I'm manipulating data to send the app platform what is the closest value of the users they're sending me.

513
00:51:44,800 --> 00:51:45,920
I'm not trying to lie here.

514
00:51:46,080 --> 00:51:56,880
At the contrary, I'm trying to fix something that is broken in the sense that the platform is receiving a value that is not representative of my business value, like the default configuration is not what a representative.

515
00:51:57,440 --> 00:52:12,000
One thing I'm working on right now, for example, is that users that are not converting within 24 hours but are demonstrating a very high likelihood of retaining for a long time as a freemium user or converting later, we're going to assign a small value to it.

516
00:52:12,400 --> 00:52:19,200
So not the ten per month or ninety per year or whatever, but maybe one dollar, like, because I'm like, hmm, I like this user.

517
00:52:19,440 --> 00:52:25,280
And if I send zero, the zero event, the zero value, the platform is going to conclude that this is the kind of users I don't want.

518
00:52:25,440 --> 00:52:33,680
And maybe because they create the network effect, they create DAU, they create late conversions that are very hard for the network to assimilate.

519
00:52:34,320 --> 00:52:39,920
I'm going to have to send something that shows them, hey, this user is really zero, but that one is actually a little something.

520
00:52:40,240 --> 00:52:47,040
The whole scheme there is, I want to tell Meta, I want to tell Google, I want to tell TikTok about what's valuable for me, because it's not natural for them.

521
00:52:47,360 --> 00:52:50,000
They just received a very primitive signal.

522
00:52:50,400 --> 00:52:58,320
I heard from Meta last week that they were working on optimizing towards different events at the same time, like for example different tiers of subscription and stuff.

523
00:52:58,480 --> 00:53:07,920
And I was very happy because it's something that I always wanted to do and that you can almost only do by engineering the signal and engineering the revenue behind it.

524
00:53:08,960 --> 00:53:15,840
So we're getting there, it's just I want to send something that is representative of value so that the platform can do the work they do the best way they can.

525
00:53:16,240 --> 00:53:23,280
And they're really, really strong at predicting which users are going to complete the action that I'm telling them.

526
00:53:23,520 --> 00:53:26,000
But if the action I'm tellinging them is wrong.

527
00:53:26,240 --> 00:53:28,000
You're running on one leg, literally.

528
00:53:28,400 --> 00:53:33,600
And that's where the whole idea of signal engineering and optimization of the data that you're sending back comes from.

529
00:53:33,920 --> 00:53:38,800
It's like, yeah, send the network something better and they're going to do a better job because they are doing a better job.

530
00:53:39,120 --> 00:53:40,320
It's you who are not doing your.

531
00:53:42,320 --> 00:53:42,640
Nice.

532
00:53:42,960 --> 00:53:59,040
So you've you've kind of gone into and given a lot of what I would guess were our kind of more advanced signal engineering practices, but let's step back and like once you get your data right or believe you have your data right, what's the low hanging fruit in signal engineering?

533
00:53:59,280 --> 00:54:00,480
Like where would you say?

534
00:54:01,200 --> 00:54:11,440
are the first kind of few steps folks should take to start changing the default mappings of how these SDKs operate by default?

535
00:54:11,680 --> 00:54:13,040
Like, what's the first step?

536
00:54:13,600 --> 00:54:16,320
So we mentioned that that's the what's the second step?

537
00:54:16,480 --> 00:54:20,480
Because the first step is monitoring that the events are passing properly, which is not a given.

538
00:54:20,800 --> 00:54:28,560
But the second step for me would be either going to what we call qualified trials or filtered trials.

539
00:54:29,360 --> 00:54:37,600
